.TH SINDOOK-SHRED 1 "2026-08-15" "sindook 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sindook-shred \- overwrite files before deleting them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sindook shred
[\fB\-n\fP \fIPASSES\fP] \fIFILE\fP...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Overwrites each \fIFILE\fP with pseudorandom data and then unlinks it,
so the plaintext is not recoverable from the deleted file through
ordinary means. Refuses symbolic links and non-regular files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-n\fP \fIPASSES\fP
number of overwrite passes to perform before unlinking
.SH CAVEATS
Shredding a file cannot destroy every trace of it. On SSDs, wear
leveling and the flash translation layer mean the overwritten blocks
are not necessarily the ones holding prior data; TRIM may make
recovery impossible or may leave a prior copy until the controller
decides otherwise. Journaling filesystems, copy-on-write filesystems
(APFS, ZFS, btrfs), snapshots, backups, and cloud sync folders may
retain earlier copies that shred cannot reach. Shredding a file also
does not destroy copies an attacker already made. For stronger
guarantees, use full-disk encryption before data is written, or
physically destroy the medium.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sindook shred old-plaintext.txt
sindook shred -n 3 *.docx
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sindook (1)
